1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
There's something else we need to go over when it comes to claud.md.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
You write a rule in your claud.md, and then you wonder which file actually wins when there

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
are several of them.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
A home file, a repo file, maybe an admin policy on top.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson maps the hierarchy, how those files load, and which one takes precedence.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's break it down.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Claud.md isn't one file.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,000
It is a stack of them.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
And there are four scopes worth knowing.

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
First, managed policy.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
An administrator deploys it machine-wide, it loads first, and no user can exclude it.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Second, the user scope.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
That is your personal file in your home config directory, and it applies across all of your projects.

14
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Third, the project scope.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
That file sits at the repository root, or the project config folder, and it is committed,

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
so the whole team shares it. Fourth, local.

17
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That is a personal git-ignored file scoped to one project, something that stays with

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
you and never gets committed.

19
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Four scopes, from the broadest machine policy down to your own private notes.

20
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So how do all those files come together at launch?

21
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Claud walks up the directory tree from wherever you started it.

22
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And it gathers every claud.md it finds along the way.

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Here is the part to hold on to.

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Those files are concatenated, not overwriting each other.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Nothing gets erased.

26
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
They are ordered from the file system root downward toward your working directory.

27
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Which means the most specific file, the one closest to where you launched, is read last.

28
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
And reading order is the whole game here.

29
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
What comes last sits nearest the model's attention and carries the most weight when it is time to act.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So broad context loads early.

31
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
And your most specific rules land at the end.

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Let's lock in three facts about how the layering works, because they explain everything else.

33
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
First, concatenation.

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Every file Claud discovers gets stacked together into context.

35
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:07,000
No file wipes out another.

36
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
They simply combine.

37
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Second, root down order.

38
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Files load from the file system root down to your working directory.

39
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So the closest file lands last in the stack.

40
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Third, composition.

41
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The design intent is layering, not strict override.

42
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And here is the honest caveat.

43
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
If rules genuinely contradict each other, Claud may pick either one.

44
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
So you shouldn't lean on precedence to resolve a real conflict.

45
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Keep those three in mind, and the behavior stops feeling mysterious.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Files combine, order matters, and clean rules never fight.

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Let's compare the two scopes you will touch most, user and project.

48
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Your user-level file is the personal baseline that follows you across every project.

49
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Broad defaults you want in every repository belong there.

50
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Your general preferences, your habits.

51
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
The project-level file is different.

52
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
It sits in the repo, and because it is read after your user file, it tends to win in practice

53
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
when the two disagree.

54
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Team conventions for this specific codebase live there.

55
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The build commands, the patterns, everyone should follow.

56
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
So the mental rule is simple.

57
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The project file tends to carry the day because it is read later in the stack, but nothing

58
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
enforces that, so don't rely on it.

59
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Design the conflicts out instead.

60
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Personal defaults set the floor, project rules refine them for the work at hand.

61
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Now a piece people miss constantly.

62
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Not every Claud.md loads when your session starts.

63
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Rules that sit below your working directory, down in subfolders, aren't injected at launch.

64
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Instead a nested file loads only when Claude actually reads a file inside that folder.

65
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Think about why that is useful.

66
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
It keeps deep folder-specific rules out of your context until the moment they are relevant.

67
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
You don't pay for them, and you aren't distracted by them while you work elsewhere in the tree.

68
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So a rule you place in a subdirectory scopes itself naturally to the work happening right there.

69
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
When Claude moves into that part of the codebase, the matching guidance shows up on demand,

70
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and not a moment before.

71
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Claude.md can also pull in other files through imports, and there are three things to know.

72
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
First, the syntax.

73
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
An at-path reference written with the at sign and a file path expands that other file into

74
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
context at launch, resolved relative to the importing file.

75
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Second, the depth limit.

76
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Imports can chain one file pulling in another, but the recursion stops at a maximum of four hops.

77
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
That ceiling keeps a runaway chain from loading half your repository.

78
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Third, the cost.

79
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Imported files load into context just like inline rules do.

80
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So imports help you organize your rules across several files, but they don't save you any tokens.

81
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Everything imported still spends its full weight in context.

82
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Let's talk about the scope that sits above everything, managed policy.

83
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
This is a Claude.md an organization deploys machine-wide, and it loads before both the

84
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
user and project files.

85
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
The important property is that no user can remove it.

86
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Managed can embed its rules directly through a managed setting, so the policy travels with

87
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
the machine rather than any single repo.

88
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And while there is an exclusion mechanism that lets you drop ancestor files you don't

89
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
want in a monorepo, it can never touch the managed policy file.

90
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
That one is always in, so when a company needs a standing rule to hold across every project

91
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
and every developer, no exceptions.

92
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Managed policy is exactly how that rule sticks.

93
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Let's turn the hierarchy into a design habit, because knowing the order is only half the value.

94
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Since these files layer, instead of hard overriding, the safest approach is to avoid conflicts

95
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
in the first place.

96
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:17,000
So put your broad, personal defaults at the user level, where they follow you everywhere.

97
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Put the team's rules in the committed project file, where everyone on the codebase inherits them.

98
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And keep narrow, folder-specific rules in subdirectory files, where they load only when

99
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
that work is active.

100
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Notice what this buys you.

101
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
When each rule is scoped to exactly where it belongs, the different layers rarely contradict each other.

102
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
You almost never have to rely on precedence to break a tie, because you designed the tie

103
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
out from the start.

104
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Let's look at the big picture.

105
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
The hierarchy layers your cloud.md files from broad to specific.

106
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Managed policy loads first and can't be excluded.

107
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Files concatenate, and the most specific one is read last.

108
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Keep in mind, project tends to win over user, because it's read later.

109
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
But don't count on it.

110
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
It's not always the case.

111
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Design for composition, and let scope decide.

112
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
That wraps up the cloud.md precedence hierarchy.

113
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
I'll catch you in the next one.

